%I A399934 #10 Sep 23 2026 18:29:17
%S A399934 6,14,23,35,49,64,82,102,123,147,173,200,230,262,295,331,369,408,450,
%T A399934 494,539,587,637,688,742,798,855,915,977,1040,1106,1174,1243,1315,
%U A399934 1389,1464,1542,1622,1703,1787,1873,1960,2050,2142,2235,2331,2429,2528,2630
%N A399934 Six times the maximum area of the convex hull of a polyhex with n cells, where each hexagonal cell has area 1.
%C A399934 A polyhex with n cells is an edge-connected set of n cells of the regular hexagonal tiling. The area of the convex hull of such a set is always a multiple of 1/6, so a(n) is an integer.
%C A399934 S. Kurz conjectured in 2008 that the maximum area is at most floor(n^2 + 14*n/3 + 1)/6. The formula below proves this conjecture and shows that the bound is attained exactly when n is not divisible by 3 (see the Gaur link).
%C A399934 The maximum is attained by a path of n cells made of three straight runs in the three lattice directions, with run lengths as equal as possible. For n <= 12 an exhaustive search shows that this is the only maximiser up to rotation and reflection.
%C A399934 The upper bound a(n) <= ceiling(n^2 + 14*n/3) is formally verified in Lean 4 with Mathlib (see the GitHub link).
%C A399934 The analogous sequence for polyominoes is 2*n + floor((n-1)/2)*floor(n/2), which is twice the maximum area (Bezdek, Brass and Harborth; Kurz).
%H A399934 Pragyaan Gaur, <a href="https://github.com/pragyaangaur/Polyhex-Hull">Lean 4 formalization of the upper bound</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%H A399934 Sascha Kurz, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0702786">Convex hulls of polyominoes</a>, arXiv:math/0702786 [math.CO], 2007; Beiträge zur Algebra und Geometrie 49 (2008), 125-136.
%H A399934 Veronika Steffanová, <a href="https://dspace.cuni.cz/handle/20.500.11956/67578">Extremal Polyominoes</a>, Master's thesis, Charles University, Prague, 2015.
%H A399934 <a href="/index/Rec#order_05">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (2,-1,1,-2,1).
%F A399934 a(n) = ceiling(n^2 + 14*n/3).
%F A399934 a(n) = floor((3*n^2 + 14*n + 2)/3).
%F A399934 a(n) = 2*a(n-1) - a(n-2) + a(n-3) - 2*a(n-4) + a(n-5) for n > 5.
%F A399934 G.f.: x*(6 + 2*x + x^2 - 3*x^3)/((1 - x)^3*(1 + x + x^2)).
%F A399934 E.g.f.: exp(-x/2)*(3*exp(3*x/2)*(1 + 17*x + 3*x^2) - 3*cos(sqrt(3)*x/2) - sqrt(3)*sin(sqrt(3)*x/2))/9. - _Stefano Spezia_, Sep 16 2026
%e A399934 a(1) = 6 because a single hexagon has area 1.
%e A399934 a(2) = 14 because the hull of two adjacent hexagons has area 7/3.
%e A399934 a(3) = 23: the best three cells form a bent path with hull area 23/6. A straight line of three cells has hull area 22/6, and a triangle of three cells has hull area 21/6.
%t A399934 Table[Ceiling[n^2 + 14 n/3], {n, 1, 49}]
%o A399934 (PARI) a(n) = ceil(n^2 + 14*n/3)
%o A399934 (Python)
%o A399934 def a(n): return (3*n*n + 14*n + 2)//3
%Y A399934 Cf. A001207 (number of fixed polyhexes), A000228 (number of free polyhexes), A122133 (polyominoes with maximum convex hull).
%K A399934 nonn,easy,new
%O A399934 1,1
%A A399934 _Pragyaan Gaur_, Sep 16 2026
